  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Using Block: Polynomdivision 4.grades 02 (1 double, 2 simple real roots)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find all roots of the polynomial \(p(x)\). Potential multiple roots only have to be listed once.</p>
<p>\[p(x) = {@poly@}.\]</p>
<p>
[[repeat id="root"]]
\(x_{[[repeatindex/]]} = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]<br>
[[/repeat]]
[[repeatbutton title="Add another root" repeat_ids="root" save_state="state1" /]]
[[input:state1]] [[validation:state1]]
</p>
<p><strong>Remark</strong>: You can't delete input fields. If you add more fields than you require in the end, you can simply leave them blank/empty. They will not be evaluated.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Solution</strong>: This is a polynomial of degree four, and you probably do not know a formula for its roots.</p>
<p>In this case, try to guess a root.  Here one quickly finds that \(x = {@zero1@}\) is a root of \(p(x)\), i.e. \(p({@zero1@}) = 0\) (try the integers from \(-2\) to \(2\) first).</p>
<p>If \(x_1 = {@zero1@}\) is a root, then \((x-x_1) = ({@x-zero1@})\) is a factor of the polynomial.  Divide \(p(x)\) by it, and find the roots of the quotient in the same way.</p>
<p>The roots are \({@zero1@}\) (a double root), \({@zero2@}\) and \({@zero3@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0</penalty>
